{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>Vagaban.</b> שִׁיּוּט no es sino un término de pasear, אישבנ״ייר — sin esfuerzo." "<b>Y la molía en molinos etc.</b> No descendía como harina de molino de mano ni en olla ni en mortero, sino que su sabor cambiaba para la molida, para la majada y para la cocida (Sifrei)." "<b>En olla.</b> Una caldera." "<b>La crema del aceite.</b> La exudación del aceite — así lo explicó Dunash, y similar a ello: ״נֶהְפַּךְ לְשַׁדִּי בְּחַרְבֹנֵי קַיִץ״ (Salmos 32) — y la lamed es la raíz: נֶהְפַּךְ לִחְלוּחִי בְּחַרְבוֹנֵי קַיִץ. Y our Rabbis explained it as a term for breasts, but the matter of breasts is irrelevant to oil. And one cannot say לְשַׁד הַשָּׁמֶן is from the language of ״וַיִּשְׁמַן יְשֻׁרוּן״ (Deuteronomy 32), for if so the mem would be vowelized with qamatz and its accent below the mem; now that the mem is vowelized with small patach and the accent is under the shin, it is a term for oil, and the shin is vowelized with qamatz gadol and is not vowelized with small patach because it is the end of the verse. Another interpretation: לְשַׁד is notarikon — לִישׁ שֶׁמֶן דְּבַשׁ, like a dough kneaded with oil and coated with honey. And the Targum of Onkelos, who translates ״דְּלִישׁ בְּמִשְׁחָא,״ inclines toward Dunash's interpretation — for a dough kneaded with oil has in it an exudation of oil."], :book "numbers", :voice :rashi, :lang :es, :chapter 11, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:43:29.842617526Z", :translated-from :en, :model "glm-5.2"}